  "slug": "design-silicon-b452",
  "summary": "Open-source and surrogate-based tools are now reducing simulation cost in silicon and nitride photonic device design.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the replacement or streamlining of expensive full-physics simulation with purpose-built computational workflows for integrated photonic design. TARANG provides an open-source Python pipeline that takes refractive indices through to GDSII masks for silicon nitride microcomb chips, with validated dispersion. A neural network surrogate trained on double-pulse femtosecond simulations predicts optothermal observables for thin silicon films on fused silica, enabling fast design exploration. A differentiable coupled-mode-theory surrogate supports gradient-based inverse design of multimode waveguides, reducing transmission error by 17-37% relative to forward EME on TFLN. The new element across these works is the use of surrogate or end-to-end differentiable models to make design loops faster and more tractable, although the three address distinct materials platforms and physical regimes.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
